WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.938
This slide presents the logical flow of a complete
aggregation query The

00:00:04.962 --> 00:00:08.462
written order of SQL starts with SELECT but it is
often easier

00:00:08.488 --> 00:00:12.650
to reason about the query in the order shown here
FROM identifies

00:00:12.675 --> 00:00:18.200
the source data WHERE removes source rows that
should not participate GROUP

00:00:18.212 --> 00:00:24.188
BY forms the groups HAVING filters those completed
groups SELECT produces

00:00:24.212 --> 00:00:29.462
the final columns and aggregate values ORDER BY
arranges the final result

00:00:30.325 --> 00:00:35.438
This order explains many SQL rules For example WHERE
cannot refer to

00:00:35.462 --> 00:00:38.662
a same-level aggregate such as SUM quantity because
the groups have not

00:00:38.675 --> 00:00:43.250
been created yet when WHERE is evaluated HAVING can
use SUM quantity

00:00:43.262 --> 00:00:47.900
because it runs after grouping This order also helps
with performance and

00:00:47.925 --> 00:00:51.800
clarity If a row does not belong in the analysis at
all

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:56.138
filter it in WHERE before the database spends effort
grouping it When

00:00:56.150 --> 00:01:00.212
a grouped query becomes confusing rewrite the
problem as a sequence source

00:01:00.338 --> 00:01:05.050
row filter grain group filter output sort You can
even write those

00:01:05.075 --> 00:01:10.125
words as comments before filling in the SQL
Analytical SQL becomes much

00:01:10.150 --> 00:01:12.700
easier when you think of it as a pipeline rather
than one

00:01:12.712 --> 00:01:13.550
large statement
